This is a sample YODL document used to test the po4a YODL parser.

The tt(yodl) program reads one or more input files and writes one output
file.  Inline macros such as em(italic) stay inside the whole paragraph, and
so does a link(fall back to)(plainhtml) command with several arguments.

center(
    tt(yodl) em(flags) em(inputfile) em([inputfile...])
)

itemization(
    it() tt(-D) em(name): Defines em(name) as a symbol.
    it() tt(-I) em(directory): Searches include files in a directory.
)

description(
    dit(tt(-N) em(number))
    Sets the counter em(number) to the given value.
)

chapter(Using the yodl program)

Some text containing the verb(latex --output) macro, which is never
translated.

ttbegin()
Text between the ttbegin and ttend markers is reported as its own section.
ttend()

Text following the closing marker is a plain paragraph again.

A comma does not separate the arguments of a macro call, and a macro name
consists of letters only: book2(x) is therefore plain text here, not a call to
the book macro.

quote(One argument, with a comma inside it.)
